-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Gemma
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Gemma is provided under and subject to the Gemma Terms of Use found at
ai.google.dev/gemma/terms

Use of the Gemma model and its derivatives is additionally subject to the Gemma
Prohibited Use Policy at ai.google.dev/gemma/prohibited_use_policy, which is
referenced from this application's Terms of Service.

What is redistributed here, and in what form:

  data/assets/gemma3-tok.v1.bin.br   1,192,944 bytes
      A repacked, losslessly re-encoded form of the tokenizer shipped with
      google/gemma-3-4b-it. It contains the full 262,144-piece vocabulary in id
      order, the sparse piece-type table, a merge-order permutation, and the
      added-token table. The 514,906 BPE merges are NOT stored: every one of
      them is re-derived at load time from the vocabulary strings alone (an
      exact set match against Hugging Face's published merge list), so only the
      tie-ordering among equal-score pieces is carried, as a permutation.
      No model weights are redistributed, in any form, anywhere in this
      repository or in the deployed image.

  data/golden/tokenizer_golden.json  2,179,417 bytes
      4,031 (text -> token id) conformance vectors produced by running the
      Hugging Face `tokenizers` Rust implementation against the same tokenizer.
      They exist so that a re-implementation cannot silently disagree with the
      original.

Model weights (approximately 8.64 GB for google/gemma-3-4b-it) are used ONLY on
the author's local machine, during authoring, to generate passages. They are
never committed, never built into the container image, and never served.

Text generated by the Gemma model and published in data/passages/ is used in
accordance with the Gemma Terms of Use; Google claims no rights in model
outputs.


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SynthID text watermarking
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The watermarking scheme implemented here is the Tournament sampling scheme
described in "Scalable watermarking for identifying large language model
outputs" (Dathathri et al., Nature, 2024), as implemented in Hugging Face
`transformers` (`SynthIDTextWatermarkLogitsProcessor` /
`SynthIDTextWatermarkDetector`).

The 30 watermark keys used by this project are the canonical PUBLISHED key set
that appears identically in Google DeepMind's `synthid-text` repository, the
Hugging Face research-projects configuration, and the published Hub detector
configurations. They are shipped to the browser deliberately.

  This project demonstrates a REAL watermark. It does not, and must not be
  described as demonstrating a SECURE one. Publishing the keys destroys the
  watermark's security and is the entire point: anyone can verify that the
  detector reading on screen is honest by recomputing it, including with stock
  `transformers`.


